    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-b0b18437-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "70a45651f30bf6537e377bbe9a8d4b4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1196,
    "chunk_text": "Why re-introduce the death penalty now?\n\nThe question of whether we need the death penalty or not has once again come to the limelight. A news report read that the Welikada gallows are ready if the death penalty is going to be imposed. After 1976, Sri Lanka didn\u2019t witness any executions taking place. The final judicial execution took place on June 23, 1976. The prisoner executed was\u00a0 convicted criminal Embilipitiye Chandradasa, also known as \u201cHonda Papuwa\u201d, and not D.J. Siripala, widely known as \u201cMaru Sira\u201d, as it is widely believed. Maru Sira was hanged in 1975.\u00a0 Even though there were attempts to bring back the death penalty, none of these attempts saw light of day. But the incumbent government has proposed to introduce death penalty for drug offenders. The debate about violation of human rights has taken a strong stand,\u00a0 and as a result over 110 countries have abolished the death penalty. Sri Lanka predominantly follows Buddhist ideologies. Many have opposed the death penalty to support the idea that Sri Lanka should practice metta and compassion towards all beings. In this context,\u00a0 it doesn\u2019t seem appropriate to bring back this archaic law back into effect."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-43be63a4",
