    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-d7b7c970-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "be69f1c80c33b3a0710c71d4632583b2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "Can the NPP implement  the death  penalty  again?\n\nSri Lanka has not carried out any death row execution since 1976\n\nThe NPP\u2019s move to re-introduce the death penalty -- going against the first policy declaration of the JVP drafted by its founder leader Rohana Wijeweera and some others in 1973 - would never materilise, given the threat it poses to the country\u2019s economy\n\nIt took almost two weeks for the Catholic Church to express its stance on the death penalty imposed on former Defence Secretary Hemasiri Fernando and former Inspector General of Police (IGP) Pujith Jayasundara over the 2019 Easter Sunday terrorist attacks.\n\nThe Muslim terrorist group that carried out the series of suicide bombings on that fateful day targeted mainly Catholics who were praying in three churches and holidaying in three luxury hotels.\n\nWas it the Daily Mirror article published on August 6, titled \u2018Are the Easter Sunday victims happy with the death sentences?\u2019, that prompted Cardinal Malcolm Ranjith to break the silence on the death penalty, 11 days after it was imposed on two senior officials?\n\nWhile expressing full confidence in the independence of Sri Lanka\u2019s judiciary to deliver justice, the Cardinal stated that the Catholic Church cannot accept or agree with the imposition of the death penalty, even for those responsible for the 2019 Easter Sunday bombings. Citing paragraph 2267 of the Catechism of the Catholic Church, he emphasised that capital punishment violates human dignity.\n\nOn the same day (Monday, August 10) the Cardinal released the statement informing the Catholic Church\u2019s stance on capital punishment, interestingly,\u00a0 the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government announced that it considers implementing the death penalty for drug-related offences as part of a tougher response to the growing threat posed by narcotics."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-d7b7c970",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d7b7c970",
    "date_str": "2026-08-13",
    "description": "Can the NPP implement  the death  penalty  again?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Can-the-NPP-implement-the-death-penalty-again/172-348019",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
