    "chunk_text": "More than\u00a0 11,000 MiG-21 variants were built worldwide. Additionally, India and Czechoslovakia built 851 more under license. China made thousands of license-built reverse-engineered variants such as Chengdu J-7 and these were sold to roughly 60 countries. It was ideal for third world air forces, and cheaper to buy and maintain than any Western interceptor. It could fly from rough, short air fields while its American counterparts required the finest concrete. Elegantly simple, it was designed to get off the ground fast, approach the target quickly and destroy it. The skies over North Vietnam amply proved its abilities.\n\nBut nothing of the kind has been reported from any of the approximately 60 air forces worldwide which flew the MiG-21. If the aircraft was at fault, what happened in India should have happened elsewhere, too. Therefore, the IAF crashes may be attributed to maintenance or assembly issues, though this too, is only conjecture. The fact remains that the IAF chose to fly its MiG-21s year after year despite the crashes. The final, moving farewell ceremony in 2025 made it look as if the IAF was almost reluctant to let the MiG-21 retire."
