    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ba681075819a847c036eb08f23790351",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "He was born on November 9, 1899, in the Malamulla area of \u200b\u200bPanadura, under the name Malalage George Peiris Malalasekera. His father was the renowned indigenous physician, Ayurvedic physician Malalage Siyadoris Peiris Malalasekera.\n\nThe reason for this was that, as it was an English school, students were strictly prohibited from speaking or writing in Sinhala; this was a rule enforced in English schools during the era of British missionary administration. He responded to the criticism he once faced for upholding his Sinhala identity by creating a Sinhala-English dictionary that combined the language of the British with the Sinhala language.\n\nAfter receiving his education at that school from 1907 to 1917, Mr. Gunapala enrolled at the Ceylon Medical College to qualify as a doctor, but his medical education was cut short due to his father\u2019s death.\n\nSubsequently, in 1919, he enrolled at the University of London as an external student and obtained a Bachelor of Arts (BA) degree with First Class Honours, specialising in Greek, Latin, and French.\n\nHe then enrolled at the University of London and, in 1925, demonstrated his academic excellence to the international community by simultaneously obtaining both a Master of Arts (MA) and a Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Oriental Languages \u200b\u200bthrough the London School of Oriental Studies.\n\nInspired by the Buddhist revival led by Anagarika Dharmapala, he began using the name \u2018Gunapala Piyasena\u2019 in place of his foreign names, \u2018George\u2019 and \u2018Pieris\u2019, and subsequently became known as G. P. Malalasekera. Subsequently, he joined the staff of Ananda College, Colombo, and, through successive promotions, served as the college\u2019s Deputy Principal and Acting Principal. Later, he was appointed as the first registered Principal of Nalanda College, Colombo, and elevated the institution to a prominent position within the country\u2019s education system."
  },
