    "md5": "1d8554685b8807d94517afa1f2fd6571",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1630,
    "chunk_text": "On one hand, sidelining abortion as a human rights issue and, on the other, viewing it through a clinical lens, pose challenges to legal reform. The most recent proposal by three medical colleges in 2025 exemplifies this. The proposal represents a setback in the legal reform trajectory of abortion, as it recognises unviable foetal abnormality as the only ground for a medical termination of pregnancy. This discussion thus far has excluded broader consultation with key stakeholders, including civil society organisations and the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs. The lack of consultation and the exclusion of women from the decision-making process, which undermine good governance and accountability, pose another challenge to achieving a breakthrough for legal reform in the country.\n\nWhile successive governments have promoted the concept of reform, there is now a clear opportunity to translate these ideas into actionable initiatives that can lead to meaningful change.\n\nSecondly, it is critical to renew national consultations to legalise abortion with key stakeholders and draft legal amendments based on recent empirical evidence. Although abortion is illegal in Sri Lanka, both the law and enforcement practices send mixed signals. For instance, while abortion is prohibited and carries severe penalties, authorities often turn a blind eye to operational clinics that provide such services. Furthermore, women are immune from prosecution and are encouraged to seek treatment for complications arising from abortions, recognising the adverse effects on maternal mortality as well as physical and psychological health."
