    "description": "The holy and not\u2011so\u2011holy sides of the Mecca pact",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/The-holy-and-notsoholy-sides-of-the-Mecca-pact/172-348076",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786641854.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-202d0068-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "78a247210ddd1e1d0889967e2d54c8f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "The holy and not\u2011so\u2011holy sides of the Mecca pact\n\nFrom left: Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman and Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif holding copies of the Mecca Pact after they signed the joint defence agreement in Mecca and August 7. Credit: Facebook/Shehbaz Sharif\n\nAs tensions continued to build in West Asia, history was made on the Muslim holy day of Friday, August 7, 2026, in the sacred city of Mecca, where three of the most powerful Islamic nations signed what most analysts have since described as a deal for a Muslim NATO.\n\nThe choice of Mecca as the venue for the leaders of Turkey, Saudi Arabia, and Pakistan was both significant and deliberate, for the holy city symbolises the Qur\u2019anic call for Muslim unity \u2014 a call that has remained elusive since the passing of the Prophet Muhammad 1,437 Hijri years ago.\n\nIf Muslim unity is what the \u2018Mecca Joint Defence Agreement\u2019 represents, does the accord between the three Sunni\u2011majority countries portray Sunni solidarity while excluding Shiites, who comprise 15 per cent of the world\u2019s two billion Muslims? The three countries, however, have rejected attempts to frame the deal in sectarian terms.\n\nNow rewind history to the early 20th century, when World War I broke out. There was no Saudi Arabia then. Ottoman Sultan Mehmed V declared jihad, or holy war, calling on the world\u2019s Muslims to join his empire\u2019s fight against the Allies, led by Britain. Instead of responding to the call, many Arabs rebelled. Wahhabi imams such as Sulaiman ibn Sahman from the Najd province in present\u2011day Saudi Arabia issued fatwas to justify the rebellion in religious terms. Ibn Sahman\u2019s fatwa declared the Muslim Ottomans kafirs (infidels) and hailed the British as Ahl al\u2011Kitab (People of the Scriptures), urging Muslims to join the Britain\u2011led Allies."
  },
