    "chunk_text": "Over 200,000 disputes referred to Mediation Boards annually\n\nColombo, August 14 (Daily Mirror) - More than 200,000 disputes are referred to mediation boards across Sri Lanka each year, the Ministry of Justice and National Integration said.\n\nAddressing the 36th National Mediation Day celebration at the Sri Lanka Foundation Institute in Colombo, Minister of Justice and National Integration Harshana Nanayakkara, said the increasing number of cases and resulting delays have become a major challenge for the judicial system.\n\nHe said that mediation has helped resolve around 200,000 disputes, preventing further pressure on the courts and reducing the potential backlog of cases.\n\nThe Minister said 2026 marks a significant year for the country\u2019s mediation process, with more than Rs. 800 million allocated through the Budget for the Mediation Boards Commission. The funding will support delayed training programmes, timely appointments of mediators and the strengthening of community mediation boards at the divisional secretariat level.\n\nSpecial land mediation boards have also been established in all 25 districts, while special financial dispute mediation boards have been introduced in six districts \u2014 Colombo, Gampaha, Anuradhapura, Polonnaruwa, Kandy and Monaragala \u2014 to help reduce court-referred financial disputes.\n\nMeanwhile, a Dispute Data Management System is being implemented alongside the ongoing digitisation of the judicial system. Initial data from the special land mediation boards has already been integrated into the system.\n\nNanayakkara also expressed appreciation for more than 9,000 voluntary mediators across the country, who contribute their time and expertise without financial expectations to help resolve disputes and promote peaceful settlements."
