    "md5": "896cc87570e3ebe4c93fe35a0c401c95",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "CHARTING THE NEXT WATCH\n\n\u201cBehold the true inheritance of military service: not steel, but character; not rank, but example; not power, but trust.\u201d\n\n\u201cVictory demanded imagination, resilience, and officers and sailors prepared to think beyond established doctrine when doctrine proved insufficient.\u201d\n\n\u201cGeography has blessed Sri Lanka, but geography guarantees nothing; strategic relevance belongs not merely to those well placed, but to those well prepared.\u201d\n\nDelivering the 2026 Gen. Denis Perera Memorial Oration, Navy Commander Vice Admiral Damian Fernando reflects on 75 years of naval inheritance, the lessons of asymmetric warfare, and the strategic imperative of preparing for tomorrow\u2019s maritime challenges\n\nIf I were to distil this evening\u2019s reflections into a single phrase, it would be \u2018Our Watch\u2019\u2014a simple concept carrying the accumulated weight of 75 years of naval service.\n\nTonight, our thoughts turn first to General Deshamanya Denis Perera\u2014a soldier and institution-builder whose legacy cannot be measured merely by appointments, honours, or ribbons. Such things adorn a career; they do not define it. General Denis Perera understood a fundamental truth: individuals are transient, institutions endure, and beneath both lies something even more enduring\u2014character. Behold the true inheritance of military service: not steel, but character; not rank, but example; not power, but trust.\n\nThe General Sir John Kotelawala Defence University, Diyathalawa Military Academy, the Women\u2019s Corps, and the Commando Regiment stand as enduring testimonials to that conviction\u2014reminders that the greatest monuments are built in minds, traditions, and standards that successive generations refuse to compromise. His legacy presents every generation with an inescapable question: Are we merely preparing for today\u2019s challenges, or are we building institutions capable of confronting tomorrow\u2019s uncertainties? No institution preserves its relevance by remaining static."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-15-dailymirrorlk-a83cf4cf",
