    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9f1fc5e668dbfb6e85025189c0521761",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "The General Sir John Kotelawala Defence University, Diyathalawa Military Academy, the Women\u2019s Corps, and the Commando Regiment stand as enduring testimonials to that conviction\u2014reminders that the greatest monuments are built in minds, traditions, and standards that successive generations refuse to compromise. His legacy presents every generation with an inescapable question: Are we merely preparing for today\u2019s challenges, or are we building institutions capable of confronting tomorrow\u2019s uncertainties? No institution preserves its relevance by remaining static.\n\nThat institution was tested by nearly three decades of conflict against the LTTE Sea Tigers, an adaptive maritime threat employing asymmetric tactics, unconventional thinking, and relentless innovation. There was no established doctrine or convenient precedent. Our sailors learned while fighting, adapted under fire, and evolved as the adversary changed. Courage alone could never have secured success; victory demanded imagination, resilience, and officers and sailors prepared to think beyond established doctrine.\n\nOut of necessity emerged innovation. Small-craft operations were refined, maritime intelligence matured, special operations expanded, and long-range interdiction evolved beyond our immediate waters. The campaign against the LTTE\u2019s floating logistics network became a defining maritime contribution to restoring peace. The permanent lesson was clear: institutions that learn more rapidly than their adversaries acquire an advantage which no treaty can confer and no budget can purchase.\n\nWhen the guns fell silent in 2009, our responsibilities did not diminish; they expanded. Today, our maritime responsibilities extend far beyond conventional defence to encompass IUU fishing, illegal migration, narcotics smuggling, human trafficking, maritime terrorism, environmental crime, humanitarian assistance, and regional cooperation."
  },
  {
