    "chunk_text": "Strengthen criminal justice system before reintroducing death penalty: Criminal Defence Attorney\n\nColombo, August 15 (Daily Mirror) - A senior lawyer specialising in criminal cases and appeals has stressed that Sri Lanka should first strengthen its criminal justice system before considering the reintroduction of the death penalty, warning that an irreversible punishment carries grave risks if investigative and judicial safeguards are inadequate.\n\nSenior Counsel Darshana Kuruppu, in a Facebook post, said the debate over reintroducing capital punishment had once again come to the forefront amid public demands for severe punishment for serious crimes.\n\nHe acknowledged that victims and their families deserved justice and that those responsible for serious offences must be held accountable. However, he said the death penalty was fundamentally different from other forms of punishment because wrongful imprisonment could, at least in principle, be corrected, whereas an execution could never be reversed.\n\nKuruppu said his own professional experience had demonstrated the importance of reliable scientific evidence in criminal trials.\n\nHe said he had called and presented DNA evidence in two murder trials. In one of those cases, an extremely poor accused person who could not afford private legal representation had initially been convicted by the High Court and sentenced to death.\n\nThe Court of Appeal subsequently ordered a retrial. At the retrial, appearing for the accused, he presented DNA evidence, which played an important role in establishing the circumstances of the case. The High Court ultimately acquitted the accused and released him.\n\n\u201cThis experience taught me a very important lesson,\u201d he said, stressing that if proper scientific evidence was not identified, collected and presented during a criminal investigation, an innocent person could potentially be convicted of a serious crime."
  },
