    "md5": "527e71abd1f3b75fe8a169f598e361e5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1950,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis experience taught me a very important lesson,\u201d he said, stressing that if proper scientific evidence was not identified, collected and presented during a criminal investigation, an innocent person could potentially be convicted of a serious crime.\n\nHe said the objective of a criminal investigation should not merely be to find evidence against a suspect, but to discover the truth.\n\nEvidence supporting a suspect\u2019s innocence should also be investigated and disclosed, he said, warning against investigations that begin with an assumption of guilt and then seek only evidence to support that assumption.\n\nHe proposed the introduction of body-worn cameras for police officers, particularly those involved in arrests, searches, raids and the seizure of evidence.\n\nRecording important stages of investigations through audio and video could provide an objective account of what occurred and protect both police officers and suspects while assisting courts in determining the facts, he said.\n\nHowever, he stressed that strict procedures would be required to prevent recordings from being altered, deleted or lost, while original recordings should be securely preserved and made available to courts when required.\n\nThe senior counsel also called for allegations concerning fabricated evidence and fabricated cases to be taken seriously, particularly in cases involving drug arrests and seizures.\n\nHe said the circumstances surrounding arrests, the discovery and seizure of drugs, the handling of exhibits and their production before court should be governed by transparent and verifiable procedures.\n\n\u201cIf there is doubt about the integrity of any part of that process, the credibility of the entire prosecution case may ultimately be affected,\u201d he said, adding that the risk became even more serious when the punishment under consideration was death.\n\nKuruppu also cautioned that human errors could occur within the judicial process itself."
