    "md5": "be04df8a5b2228c90a7320e0ffc6bacb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1810,
    "chunk_text": "The higher provisioning came alongside stronger underlying income generation. At Group level, net interest income increased 6 percent to Rs. 16.17 billion, while net fee and commission income rose 29 percent to Rs. 4.19 billion. Group total operating income increased 6 percent to Rs. 23.66 billion.\n\nThe balance sheet continued to expand despite the weaker earnings. Group assets increased 7 percent from December to Rs. 920.89 billion, while deposits grew 12 percent to Rs. 630.91 billion. At the Bank level, net loans and advances increased 9 percent to Rs. 558.53 billion.\n\nThe Bank\u2019s CASA portfolio increased 14 percent from end-2025, lifting the CASA ratio to 24.99 percent, while its net interest margin stood at 3.66 percent.\n\nAsset quality also improved. The Bank\u2019s net Stage 3 impaired loan ratio declined to 3.61 percent at end-June from 4.55 percent at end-December, even as the loan portfolio expanded.\n\nPerera said the improvement was achieved \u201ceven as we strengthened our prudential buffers\u201d, with the bank maintaining a selective lending approach and tighter risk management.\n\nCapital ratios eased as the balance sheet expanded. The Bank\u2019s Tier 1 capital ratio declined to 11.947 percent from 13.550 percent at end-December, while the total capital ratio edged down to 15.707 percent from 15.933 percent. DFCC is also completing regulatory and administrative formalities for a Basel III-compliant Tier II debenture issue of up to Rs. 15 billion.\n\nDFCC\u2019s balance sheet is set to expand further in the second half following the completion of its acquisition of Standard Chartered Bank\u2019s Wealth and Retail Banking business in Sri Lanka, effective August 1.\n\nThe transaction added about 50,000 customer accounts and around 260 employees and expanded DFCC\u2019s network to 139 locations."
  },
