    "md5": "6146bc4e0cd1b9895e3c7389925442b1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "No anchor, no accountability as economists blast Central Bank over two-year inflation miss\n\nReal interest rates in SL became among the highest and most volatile in the region during the two-year inflation undershoot, economists say\n\nDespite a legal requirement to explain sustained target misses to parliament, that oversight has \u201ccompletely stalled,\u201d notes Verit\u00e9 Research\u2019s Executive Director\n\nThe Central Bank of Sri Lanka\u2019s handling of monetary policy came under sustained fire at a panel hosted by the Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council, with economists questioning the institution\u2019s competence, the near-total absence of parliamentary accountability, and the damage inflicted on debt sustainability and private sector growth by two years of missed inflation targets.\n\nLegally bound to keep headline inflation within a 3-7 percent band around a 5 percent target, the Central Bank instead let it undershoot for eight consecutive quarters, often hovering at 1-2 percent and slipping as low as -4.2 percent in February 2025, before the target was finally breached from the other direction this year.\n\nSpeaking at the panel discussion \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Future - Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges,\u201d organised by the Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council of the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce in Colombo last week, Verit\u00e9 Research Executive Director Dr. Nishan de Mel said, \u201cOnce you get into a gazetted agreement with the government to deliver a certain level of inflation, you have to meet that. There is no management discretion here. These are simple facts of accountability and competence.\u201d He likened the lapse to a statutory check that exists but has gone unused: \u201cIn the Bank of England, as in Sri Lanka, we\u2019ve set up a device that says if you miss it by a certain amount, you must go to Parliament and explain why you did it. We haven\u2019t been able to get Parliament to ask good enough questions to rectify the problem, so it keeps getting missed for eight quarters in a row.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
