    "md5": "0f9b035fe869885008b5113dbe35dddc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1831,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking at the panel discussion \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Future - Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges,\u201d organised by the Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council of the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce in Colombo last week, Verit\u00e9 Research Executive Director Dr. Nishan de Mel said, \u201cOnce you get into a gazetted agreement with the government to deliver a certain level of inflation, you have to meet that. There is no management discretion here. These are simple facts of accountability and competence.\u201d He likened the lapse to a statutory check that exists but has gone unused: \u201cIn the Bank of England, as in Sri Lanka, we\u2019ve set up a device that says if you miss it by a certain amount, you must go to Parliament and explain why you did it. We haven\u2019t been able to get Parliament to ask good enough questions to rectify the problem, so it keeps getting missed for eight quarters in a row.\u201d\n\n\u201cWhen the government pays high interest and there is no inflation, what you call the real interest rates in Sri Lanka became amongst the highest in the region and also became extremely volatile,\u201d de Mel said, arguing the state ended up paying massive real interest costs on its domestic debt as a result. He was equally critical of how the Central Bank read the shocks driving the miss in both directions.\n\n\u201cWe got it wrong on both ends. We had a sustained negative supply-side shock that we didn\u2019t respond to by reducing interest rates, which we should have, to stimulate demand. But when we had a negative supply shock that\u2019s basically transient, we intervened by jacking up interest rates 100 basis points,\u201d he said - a critique that lands squarely on the Central Bank\u2019s May 2026 decision to raise its Overnight Policy Rate by exactly 100 basis points, to 8.75 percent, in response to the current energy-driven inflation spike."
  },
