    "chunk_size_bytes": 1692,
    "chunk_text": "The blind spot, he argued, is statistical as much as political. Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty rate more than doubled from 14.3 percent to nearly 30 percent during the crisis\u2014a steeper deterioration than in other debt-distressed nations\u2014yet the state has not published an official poverty measure since. \u201cWhat you fail to measure, you fail to focus on,\u201d de Mel said, noting that roughly 92 percent of formal private sector employees earn less than Rs. 100,000 a month.\n\nDe Mel linked that same concentration of advantage to Sri Lanka\u2019s high real interest rates, which he said quietly transfer wealth from wage earners to capital holders. \u201cWhen you have high interest rates and no inflation, the only people that benefit from it are people like us who have large savings, can leverage and borrow, and we make money for nothing,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cWhile all the people who are living at subsistence, are just managing on wages, are paying higher taxes - 18 percent VAT - so the government can collect that money and pay us the high real interest rates. This is a racket in which people like us benefit enormously on the backs of those who see very little benefit from the economy.\u201d\n\nBoth economists urged corporate leaders and trade chambers to move beyond narrow lobbying and push instead for a genuine national development strategy - one that also addresses the brain drain hollowing out businesses.\n\n\u201cOne of the reasons you can\u2019t hire competent people at reasonable prices is everyone who is capable wants to leave the country, and then you\u2019re left in trouble,\u201d de Mel said. \u201cNarrow interests that are constantly pursued and promoted are harming those narrow interests itself.\u201d"
